WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.569
Good logging begins with the questions the team needs to answer.

2
00:00:03.569 --> 00:00:05.618
Did authentication succeed or fail?

3
00:00:05.618 --> 00:00:06.676
Was access denied?

4
00:00:06.676 --> 00:00:08.857
Did an administrator change a setting?

5
00:00:08.857 --> 00:00:13.418
Did a payment, order, approval, deployment, or sensitive state change
complete?

6
00:00:13.418 --> 00:00:15.070
Did validation reject input?

7
00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:16.524
Did a dependency time out?

8
00:00:16.524 --> 00:00:19.168
Did a background job retry or fail permanently?

9
00:00:19.168 --> 00:00:21.878
Did rate limiting or abuse protection activate?

10
00:00:21.878 --> 00:00:28.752
Logs should support concrete operational and security questions rather
than collect data simply because it is available.

11
00:00:28.752 --> 00:00:31.925
Security-relevant events deserve intentional design.

12
00:00:31.925 --> 00:00:44.681
Authentication events, authorization decisions, sensitive state changes,
administrative actions, account recovery, data export, permission changes,
key lifecycle events, and unusual failure patterns should be visible.

13
00:00:44.681 --> 00:00:53.869
These events should describe who or what acted at an appropriate level,
which component handled the event, what outcome occurred, and what safe
reason code applies.

14
00:00:53.869 --> 00:00:57.306
They should not require full sensitive payloads to be useful.

15
00:00:57.306 --> 00:01:02.461
Request and response metadata can be valuable, but full request bodies are
risky by default.

16
00:01:02.461 --> 00:01:11.649
Bodies may contain credentials, tokens, personal data, health information,
payment details, private messages, documents, or customer-specific
business data.

17
00:01:11.649 --> 00:01:13.764
Response bodies may contain the same.

18
00:01:13.764 --> 00:01:21.563
If a team needs payload logging for a narrow troubleshooting case, it
should be time-bounded, access-controlled, redacted, and reviewed.

19
00:01:21.563 --> 00:01:25.066
The normal logging path should assume payloads are sensitive.

20
00:01:25.066 --> 00:01:27.974
Deciding what to log is also deciding what not to log.

21
00:01:27.974 --> 00:01:33.923
Logs should answer operational questions without becoming a shadow
database of user content and secrets.

22
00:01:33.923 --> 00:01:41.723
A concise structured event with a safe identifier, outcome, component, and
correlation ID is often more useful than a large free-text dump.

23
00:01:41.723 --> 00:01:48.200
The best logging designs make investigation easier while reducing how much
sensitive data must be protected later.
